{"seed":{"mim":"613581","entry_type":"phenotype","prefix":"#","title":"RETINITIS PIGMENTOSA 56","alt_titles":[],"symbol":"RP56","gene_symbols":[],"seed_pmids":[],"max_year":2014},"nodes":[{"id":"entry","type":"Entry","label":"RETINITIS PIGMENTOSA 56","mim":"613581","entry_type":"phenotype"},{"id":"section:description","type":"Section","label":"description"},{"id":"section:nomenclature","type":"Section","label":"nomenclature"},{"id":"section:cloning_expression","type":"Section","label":"cloning_expression"},{"id":"section:gene_structure","type":"Section","label":"gene_structure"},{"id":"section:clinical_features","type":"Section","label":"clinical_features"},{"id":"section:other_features","type":"Section","label":"other_features"},{"id":"section:biochemical_features","type":"Section","label":"biochemical_features"},{"id":"section:inheritance","type":"Section","label":"inheritance"},{"id":"section:cytogenetics","type":"Section","label":"cytogenetics"},{"id":"section:mapping","type":"Section","label":"mapping"},{"id":"section:heterogeneity","type":"Section","label":"heterogeneity"},{"id":"section:gene_function","type":"Section","label":"gene_function"},{"id":"section:molecular_genetics","type":"Section","label":"molecular_genetics"},{"id":"section:genotype_phenotype_correlations","type":"Section","label":"genotype_phenotype_correlations"},{"id":"section:pathogenesis","type":"Section","label":"pathogenesis"},{"id":"section:diagnosis","type":"Section","label":"diagnosis"},{"id":"section:clinical_management","type":"Section","label":"clinical_management"},{"id":"section:population_genetics","type":"Section","label":"population_genetics"},{"id":"section:evolution","type":"Section","label":"evolution"},{"id":"section:animal_model","type":"Section","label":"animal_model"},{"id":"section:history","type":"Section","label":"history"},{"id":"paper:25085631","type":"Paper","label":"Meunier et al. (2014)","pmid":"25085631","year":2014,"content_level":"abstract","relevance":"background","title":"Frequency and clinical pattern of vitelliform macular dystrophy caused by mutations of interphotoreceptor matrix IMPG1 and IMPG2 genes."},{"id":"paper:20673862","type":"Paper","label":"Bandah-Rozenfeld et al. (2010)","pmid":"20673862","year":2010,"content_level":"abstract","relevance":"primary","title":"Mutations in IMPG2, encoding interphotoreceptor matrix proteoglycan 2, cause autosomal-recessive retinitis pigmentosa."},{"id":"finding:20673862:1","type":"Finding","label":"Using autozygosity mapping, Bandah-Rozenfeld et al. (2010) identified 2 families, each with 3 affected sibs with retinitis pigmentosa. The affected sibs shared large overlapping homozygous regions on chromosome 3 that contained the IMPG2 gene.","pmid":"20673862","section":"mapping"},{"id":"gene:IMPG2","type":"Gene","label":"IMPG2"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0000510","type":"Phenotype","label":"Rod-cone dystrophy","hpo_id":"HP:0000510"},{"id":"inh:AR","type":"Inheritance","label":"Autosomal recessive","hpo_id":"HP:0000007"},{"id":"finding:20673862:2","type":"Finding","label":"In 3 affected sibs of Iraqi Jewish ancestry with autosomal recessive retinitis pigmentosa, Bandah-Rozenfeld et al. (2010) found a homozygous nonsense mutation in the IMPG2 gene. The mutation cosegregated with the disease in the family.","pmid":"20673862","section":"molecular_genetics"},{"id":"variant:IMPG2:raw:nonsense mutation","type":"Variant","label":"IMPG2 nonsense mutation","gene":"IMPG2","keys":["raw:nonsense mutation"],"omim_name":"IMPG2"},{"id":"finding:20673862:3","type":"Finding","label":"In a Dutch family with autosomal recessive retinitis pigmentosa, Bandah-Rozenfeld et al. (2010) found a homozygous 1.8-kb genomic deletion in the IMPG2 gene that removes exon 9. The deletion leads to the loss of 7 amino acids in a conserved SEA domain and cosegregated with the disease. In transfected COS-1 cells, the wildtype SEA domain was targeted to the plasma membrane, but the mutant lacking the 7 amino acids appeared to be retained in the endoplasmic reticulum.","pmid":"20673862","section":"molecular_genetics"},{"id":"variant:IMPG2:raw:1.8 kb genomic deletion that removes exon 9","type":"Variant","label":"IMPG2 1.8 kb genomic deletion that removes exon 9","gene":"IMPG2","keys":["raw:1.8 kb genomic deletion that removes exon 9"],"omim_name":"IMPG2"},{"id":"finding:20673862:4","type":"Finding","label":"Bandah-Rozenfeld et al. (2010) analyzed IMPG2 in 10 additional index cases of Dutch, Israeli, Italian, and Pakistani origin whose homozygous regions included IMPG2. They found 5 additional mutations: 4 nonsense mutations and 1 missense mutation that affects a highly conserved phenylalanine residue.","pmid":"20673862","section":"molecular_genetics"},{"id":"variant:IMPG2:raw:missense mutation affecting a highly conserved phenylalanine residue","type":"Variant","label":"IMPG2 missense mutation affecting a highly conserved phenylalanine residue","gene":"IMPG2","keys":["raw:missense mutation affecting a highly conserved phenylalanine residue"],"omim_name":"IMPG2"},{"id":"finding:20673862:5","type":"Finding","label":"Bandah-Rozenfeld et al. (2010) reported that most patients with IMPG2 mutations had early-onset retinitis pigmentosa, with visual-field loss and declining visual acuity that both progressed over time.","pmid":"20673862","section":"clinical_features"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0001123","type":"Phenotype","label":"Visual field defect","hpo_id":"HP:0001123"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0007663","type":"Phenotype","label":"Reduced visual acuity","hpo_id":"HP:0007663"},{"id":"finding:20673862:6","type":"Finding","label":"Bandah-Rozenfeld et al. (2010) reported that the patient with the IMPG2 missense mutation was diagnosed with maculopathy, not retinitis pigmentosa.","pmid":"20673862","section":"genotype_phenotype_correlations"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0000608","type":"Phenotype","label":"Macular degeneration","hpo_id":"HP:0000608"},{"id":"finding:25085631:1","type":"Finding","label":"Meunier et al. (2014) screened IMPG1 and IMPG2 in 49 families with vitelliform macular dystrophy who had no identified BEST1 or PRPH2 mutation. In 1 family (2 patients) with autosomal dominant vitelliform macular dystrophy, they identified a c.3230G-T mutation in IMPG2, resulting in a cys1077-to-phe (C1077F) substitution. IMPG1 was the causal gene in 3 other families. Overall, the authors estimated that IMPG1 and IMPG2 account for 8% of families negative for BEST1 and PRPH2 mutations.","pmid":"25085631","section":"molecular_genetics"},{"id":"variant:IMPG2:c.3230G>T","type":"Variant","label":"IMPG2 c.3230G>T / p.C1077F","gene":"IMPG2","keys":["c.3230G>T","p.C1077F"],"omim_name":"IMPG2, CYS1077PHE"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0007677","type":"Phenotype","label":"Vitelliform macular lesion","hpo_id":"HP:0007677"},{"id":"inh:AD","type":"Inheritance","label":"Autosomal dominant","hpo_id":"HP:0000006"},{"id":"finding:25085631:2","type":"Finding","label":"Meunier et al. (2014) reported that the 13 patients with IMPG1 or IMPG2 mutations had late-onset vitelliform macular dystrophy with moderate visual impairment (mean age of onset, 42 years; mean visual acuity, 20/40). Drusen-like lesions next to the vitelliform deposits were seen in 9 of the 13 patients. On spectral-domain OCT, the vitelliform material lay above the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE), and the RPE was well preserved with normal reflectivity. Electro-oculogram results were normal or borderline in 9 cases.","pmid":"25085631","section":"clinical_features"},{"id":"gene:IMPG1","type":"Gene","label":"IMPG1"},{"id":"hpo:HP:0030499","type":"Phenotype","label":"Macular drusen","hpo_id":"HP:0030499"}],"edges":[{"src":"paper:20673862","rel":"reports","dst":"finding:20673862:1"},{"src":"finding:20673862:1","rel":"in_section","dst":"section:mapping"},{"src":"finding:20673862:1","rel":"mentions_gene","dst":"gene:IMPG2","pmid":"20673862"},{"src":"finding:20673862:1","rel":"has_phenotype","dst":"hpo:HP:0000510","pmid":"20673862","present":true,"text":"retinitis pigmentosa"}